codeExamples.code:
let age: number = 30;
let username: string = "alice";
let isActive: boolean = true;
let scores: number[] = [10, 20, 30];

console.log(`${username} is ${age} years old. Active: ${isActive}`);
console.log("Total score:", scores.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s, 0));

let point: [number, number] = [10, 20];
// point = [10, 20, 30]; // Error: Source has 3 element(s) but target allows only 2.
// point = ["10", 20];   // Error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.

console.log(`x=${point[0]}, y=${point[1]}`);

let message = "Hello";
let count = 5;

// message = 42; // Error: Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'string'.

console.log(typeof message, typeof count);

const numbers = [1, 2, 3];
numbers.forEach((n) => {
  console.log(n.toFixed(2));
});

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both `any` and `unknown` can hold a value of any type, but they behave very differently once you try to *use* that value.

- `any` completely disables type checking for that value. You can call methods on it, access arbitrary properties, or reassign it to anything, and the compiler never complains. This defeats the purpose of using TypeScript.
- `unknown` is the type-safe counterpart. You can assign anything to an `unknown` variable, but you cannot perform any operation on it -- call a method, access a property, call it as a function -- until you first narrow its type (with `typeof`, `instanceof`, or a type guard).

```ts
function processAny(value: any) {
  value.toUpperCase(); // compiles, may crash at runtime
}

function processUnknown(value: unknown) {
  // value.toUpperCase(); // Error: Object is of type 'unknown'.
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    value.toUpperCase(); // OK, narrowed to string
  }
}
```

`unknown` forces you to prove what the type is before using it, while `any` trusts you blindly.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
An `interface` is a named contract that describes the shape of an object: which properties it has, and what type each one must be. It exists purely at compile time -- it produces no JavaScript output -- and is one of the most common ways to describe data in TypeScript.

## Declaring an interface

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function printUser(user: User): void {
  console.log(`#${user.id}: ${user.name} (${user.email})`);
}
```

Any object passed to `printUser` must have at least an `id`, `name`, and `email` with matching types. TypeScript uses **structural typing** (sometimes called "duck typing"): an object satisfies an interface as long as it has the right shape, regardless of how it was created.

```ts
const u = { id: 1, name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com" };
printUser(u); // works, u structurally matches User
```

## Nested and reused interfaces

Interfaces can reference other interfaces, letting you compose larger shapes out of smaller ones:

```ts
interface Address {
  city: string;
  zip: string;
}

interface Customer {
  name: string;
  address: Address;
}
```

## Extending interfaces

An interface can extend one or more other interfaces with the `extends` keyword, inheriting their members:

```ts
interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;
}

const d: Dog = { name: "Rex", breed: "Labrador" };
```

This makes interfaces a powerful tool for modeling hierarchies of related data shapes without duplicating property definitions.

codeExamples.code:
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function printUser(user: User): void {
  console.log(`#${user.id}: ${user.name} (${user.email})`);
}

const u = { id: 1, name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com" };
printUser(u);

interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;
}

const d: Dog = { name: "Rex", breed: "Labrador" };
console.log(`${d.name} is a ${d.breed}`);

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Real-world object shapes are rarely all-required and all-mutable. TypeScript interfaces support **optional properties** and **readonly properties** to express these variations precisely.

## Optional properties

A property marked with `?` may be omitted entirely when creating a matching object:

```ts
interface Product {
  name: string;
  price: number;
  discount?: number; // optional
}

const a: Product = { name: "Mug", price: 10 };
const b: Product = { name: "Mug", price: 10, discount: 2 };
```

Accessing `a.discount` gives `number | undefined`, so the compiler forces you to check for `undefined` before performing arithmetic on it.

## Readonly properties

A property marked `readonly` can be set when the object is first created, but not reassigned afterward:

```ts
interface Point {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
}

const p: Point = { x: 0, y: 0 };
// p.x = 5; // Error: Cannot assign to 'x' because it is a read-only property.
```

This is enforced only at compile time -- it has no effect on the emitted JavaScript -- but it is extremely useful for modeling immutable data such as configuration objects or coordinates that should never be mutated after creation.

## Combining both

Optional and readonly can be combined on the same property, and are also available on classes that implement an interface, making them a flexible tool for describing precise, safe data contracts.

codeExamples.code:
interface Product {
  name: string;
  price: number;
  discount?: number;
}

function finalPrice(p: Product): number {
  return p.discount ? p.price - p.discount : p.price;
}

const a: Product = { name: "Mug", price: 10 };
const b: Product = { name: "Mug", price: 10, discount: 2 };

console.log(finalPrice(a), finalPrice(b));

interface Point {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
}

const p: Point = { x: 0, y: 0 };
// p.x = 5; // Compile error: Cannot assign to 'x' because it is a read-only property.

console.log(`(${p.x}, ${p.y})`);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Structural typing means TypeScript compares types by their **shape** (the members they have) rather than by name or explicit declaration, as in nominal type systems like Java or C#. An object is considered compatible with an interface as long as it has all the required properties with compatible types -- it doesn't need to explicitly declare that it implements that interface.

```ts
interface Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

function logPoint(p: Point) {
  console.log(`(${p.x}, ${p.y})`);
}

const obj = { x: 1, y: 2, label: "origin" };
logPoint(obj); // OK -- obj has at least x and y
```

`obj` is never declared as a `Point`, yet it is accepted because it structurally contains compatible `x` and `y` properties. Note that extra properties are allowed when the value is referenced through a variable; TypeScript only performs stricter "excess property checking" on **object literals passed directly** to a typed parameter, e.g. `logPoint({ x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 })` would error because the literal has no corresponding assignment to narrow.

`?` marks a property as not required when creating a value of that interface type. Its type becomes a union with `undefined` when read, so the compiler forces a check (e.g. `if (obj.prop)`) before you can safely use it as its underlying type.

`readonly` marks a property as settable only at the point of object creation. After that, any direct assignment to it is a compile-time error. Crucially, `readonly` is a **compile-time-only** construct: there is zero runtime enforcement. Once compiled to JavaScript, the `readonly` keyword disappears entirely, and nothing prevents a determined caller from mutating the property via `Object.assign`, bracket notation tricks, or simply ignoring TypeScript's errors in plain JS code that consumes your typed API.

```ts
interface Config {
  readonly retries: number;
  timeout?: number;
}

const c: Config = { retries: 3 };
// c.retries = 5; // compile error only -- no runtime guard exists
```

For true runtime immutability, combine `readonly` with `Object.freeze()`, which throws (in strict mode) or silently no-ops on mutation attempts at runtime.

Yes -- this is called **declaration merging**. If you declare an interface with the same name more than once in the same scope, TypeScript merges all the declarations into a single interface containing the union of all their members.

```ts
interface Window {
  title: string;
}

interface Window {
  ts: TypeScriptAPI;
}

// Window now has both 'title' and 'ts'
```

This is primarily useful for augmenting types you don't own, such as extending the global `Window` object or adding properties to a third-party library's types without modifying its source. It is one of the key differences between `interface` and `type`: a `type` alias cannot be redeclared this way -- attempting to declare two `type` aliases with the same name in the same scope is a compile error (`Duplicate identifier`).

Declaration merging does have limits: if two merged declarations define the same property with incompatible types, the compiler raises a conflict error.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A `type` alias gives a name to any type -- not just object shapes. This makes it more general-purpose than an interface, which can only describe object (or function/class) shapes.

## Basic syntax

```ts
type UserId = number;
type Status = "pending" | "active" | "suspended";

type Point = {
  x: number;
  y: number;
};
```

`UserId` is just an alias for `number` -- it doesn't create a new, distinct type, but it does make signatures far more self-documenting than a bare `number`.

## Aliasing unions, tuples, and functions

Type aliases shine when naming types that interfaces cannot express directly:

```ts
type ID = string | number;             // union
type Coordinates = [number, number];   // tuple
type Comparator = (a: number, b: number) => number; // function signature
```

An interface cannot alias a union type (`interface ID = string | number` is invalid syntax) -- this is one of the clearest cases where `type` is the only option.

## Composing types

Type aliases can be combined with intersections (`&`) to build up larger shapes from smaller ones, similar to how `extends` works for interfaces:

```ts
type Named = { name: string };
type Aged = { age: number };
type Person = Named & Aged;

const p: Person = { name: "Sam", age: 25 };
```

Because `type` can alias *any* type expression -- primitives, unions, tuples, function signatures, and object shapes alike -- it is the more flexible of TypeScript's two naming tools.

codeExamples.code:
type Status = "pending" | "active" | "suspended";
type Comparator = (a: number, b: number) => number;

function describe(status: Status): string {
  return `Current status: ${status}`;
}

const byAscending: Comparator = (a, b) => a - b;

console.log(describe("active"));
console.log([3, 1, 2].sort(byAscending).join(","));

type Named = { name: string };
type Aged = { age: number };
type Person = Named & Aged;

const p: Person = { name: "Sam", age: 25 };
console.log(`${p.name} is ${p.age}`);

lessons.contentMarkdown:
For plain object shapes, `interface` and `type` overlap heavily -- the same shape can often be written either way. But there are real differences worth understanding before picking one as your default.

## Where they overlap

```ts
interface UserA {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

type UserB = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};
```

Both `UserA` and `UserB` behave almost identically as object shapes: both support optional and readonly properties, both can be implemented by classes, and both participate in structural typing the same way.

## Where they differ

| Capability | `interface` | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| Declaration merging (reopen later) | Yes | No |
| Can alias a union/tuple/primitive | No | Yes |
| `extends` another type | Yes (`extends`) | Yes (via `&`) |
| Implemented by a class | Yes | Yes (object-shaped only) |

Declaration merging means an `interface` can be augmented after the fact (useful for libraries and ambient global types), while a `type` alias is fixed once declared -- attempting to redeclare it is an error.

## A practical rule of thumb

A widely used convention in TypeScript codebases:

1. Default to `interface` for public object shapes (component props, API response/request bodies, class contracts) -- it reads clearly, supports `extends`, and can be merged/augmented by consumers if needed.
2. Reach for `type` when you need a union, tuple, mapped type, conditional type, or a primitive alias -- things an interface fundamentally cannot express.

Many teams adopt this split as a lint rule, but ultimately both tools are valid; consistency within a codebase matters more than which one is chosen.

codeExamples.code:
interface UserA {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

type UserB = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};

function greet(u: UserA | UserB): string {
  return `Hi ${u.name}, age ${u.age}`;
}

console.log(greet({ name: "Ada", age: 36 }));
console.log(greet({ name: "Grace", age: 40 }));

interface Greeting {
  text: string;
}

interface Greeting {
  volume: "quiet" | "loud";
}

const g: Greeting = { text: "hello", volume: "loud" };
console.log(`${g.text.toUpperCase()}! (${g.volume})`);

// The following would be a compile error if uncommented:
// type Greeting = { text: string }; // Duplicate identifier 'Greeting'.

interviewQuestions.answer:
Yes, as long as the type alias describes an object shape (not a union, primitive, or anything else a class instance couldn't structurally match). The `implements` clause works with both:

```ts
type Shape = {
  area(): number;
};

interface Drawable {
  draw(): void;
}

class Circle implements Shape, Drawable {
  constructor(private radius: number) {}
  area(): number {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }
  draw(): void {
    console.log("Drawing a circle");
  }
}
```

A class cannot, however, implement a `type` alias that is a union (e.g. `type Id = string | number`) or a primitive alias, because `implements` requires an object-shaped contract that a class instance can fulfill. In that regard, interfaces are the more conventional choice for contracts classes implement, even though object-shaped type aliases work identically.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A **union type**, written with `|`, describes a value that can be one of several types. It is one of the most frequently used features in real-world TypeScript code, since so much real data is "this or that".

## Declaring a union

```ts
let id: string | number;
id = "abc123"; // OK
id = 42;       // OK
// id = true;  // Error: Type 'boolean' is not assignable.
```

## Narrowing a union

Before you can use a member that isn't common to every branch of a union, TypeScript requires you to **narrow** it -- prove, through a runtime check, which specific member you're dealing with:

```ts
function formatId(id: string | number): string {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    return id.toUpperCase(); // id is string here
  }
  return id.toFixed(0); // id is number here
}
```

Only properties/methods shared by *every* member of the union are accessible without narrowing first.

## Union of object shapes (discriminated unions)

Unions are especially powerful when combining object shapes that share a common "tag" property, known as a **discriminated union**:

```ts
interface Circle {
  kind: "circle";
  radius: number;
}
interface Square {
  kind: "square";
  side: number;
}
type Shape = Circle | Square;

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return shape.side ** 2;
  }
}
```

Switching on `shape.kind` lets TypeScript narrow `shape` to the exact matching interface inside each `case` branch, giving full type safety with no casts.

codeExamples.code:
function formatId(id: string | number): string {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    return id.toUpperCase();
  }
  return id.toFixed(0);
}

console.log(formatId("abc123"));
console.log(formatId(42));

interface Circle {
  kind: "circle";
  radius: number;
}
interface Square {
  kind: "square";
  side: number;
}
type Shape = Circle | Square;

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return shape.side ** 2;
  }
}

console.log(area({ kind: "circle", radius: 2 }).toFixed(2));
console.log(area({ kind: "square", side: 3 }));

lessons.contentMarkdown:
An **intersection type**, written with `&`, combines multiple types into one that must satisfy all of them at once. Where a union means "one of these", an intersection means "all of these, merged together".

## Declaring an intersection

```ts
type Timestamped = {
  createdAt: Date;
};

type Named = {
  name: string;
};

type Record = Timestamped & Named;

const r: Record = {
  createdAt: new Date(),
  name: "entry-1",
};
```

`Record` requires every property from both `Timestamped` and `Named` -- it is the union of their *members*, not the union of possible values.

## Combining with function signatures

Intersections are also useful for augmenting an existing type with extra behavior, such as attaching metadata to a function:

```ts
type Loggable = {
  log(message: string): void;
};

type Service = {
  run(): void;
} & Loggable;

const service: Service = {
  run() {
    this.log("running");
  },
  log(message) {
    console.log(`[service] ${message}`);
  },
};
```

## Unions vs. intersections at a glance

- `A | B` -- a value that is *either* an `A` *or* a `B` (fewer guaranteed members, more possible shapes)
- `A & B` -- a value that is *both* an `A` *and* a `B` at once (more guaranteed members, a single combined shape)

If `A` and `B` have a property with the same name but conflicting types (e.g. `string` vs. `number`), the intersected property type becomes `never`, since no value could satisfy both simultaneously -- a useful signal that two types probably shouldn't be intersected.

codeExamples.code:
type Timestamped = {
  createdAt: Date;
};

type Named = {
  name: string;
};

type Entry = Timestamped & Named;

const r: Entry = {
  createdAt: new Date("2024-01-01"),
  name: "entry-1",
};

console.log(r.name, r.createdAt.getFullYear());

type Loggable = {
  log(message: string): void;
};

type Service = {
  run(): void;
} & Loggable;

const service: Service = {
  run() {
    this.log("running");
  },
  log(message) {
    console.log(`[service] ${message}`);
  },
};

service.run();

interviewQuestions.answer:
A discriminated union (also called a "tagged union") is a union of object types that each include a common literal-typed property -- the "discriminant" or "tag" -- whose value differs between the members. For example:

```ts
interface LoadingState { status: "loading"; }
interface SuccessState { status: "success"; data: string; }
interface ErrorState { status: "error"; message: string; }

type RequestState = LoadingState | SuccessState | ErrorState;

function render(state: RequestState): string {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "loading": return "Loading...";
    case "success": return `Data: ${state.data}`;
    case "error":   return `Error: ${state.message}`;
  }
}
```

The key benefit is that checking `state.status` inside an `if` or `switch` lets TypeScript automatically narrow `state` to the exact matching member within that branch, giving full property access with zero type assertions. It also makes the set of valid states exhaustive and explicit, so adding a new state variant later causes a compile error anywhere a `switch` fails to handle it (when combined with a `never`-typed default case), preventing an entire class of "forgot to handle a case" bugs.

An intersection type requires a value to simultaneously satisfy every constituent type. For the property `a`, that means the resulting type of `a` must be assignable to both `string` and `number` at once. Since no value in TypeScript (other than ones typed `never` itself) satisfies both constraints simultaneously, the compiler resolves the intersected property's type down to `never` -- a type with no possible values.

```ts
type X = { a: string };
type Y = { a: number };
type Z = X & Y; // { a: never }

const z: Z = { a: /* impossible to satisfy */ "x" as never };
```

In practice, this situation usually means the two types being intersected were never meant to be combined -- it's a signal of a design mistake rather than a useful type to construct deliberately. It commonly surfaces when intersecting two overlapping interfaces from different libraries that happen to share a property name with different purposes (e.g. one library's `id: string` and another's `id: number`). The fix is almost always to rename one of the conflicting properties, or to use a union instead of an intersection if the two shapes were never meant to merge into a single value.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A generic is a type parameter -- a placeholder type, conventionally named `T` -- that lets a function, interface, or class work with many different types while still preserving the relationship between its inputs and outputs.

## Why not just use `any`?

```ts
function firstAny(arr: any[]): any {
  return arr[0];
}

const result = firstAny([1, 2, 3]); // result is 'any' -- type info is lost
```

With `any`, the return type tells you nothing -- you lose all type safety on the result. A generic function preserves the connection between what goes in and what comes out:

```ts
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T {
  return arr[0];
}

const n = first([1, 2, 3]);       // n: number
const s = first(["a", "b", "c"]); // s: string
```

`T` is inferred automatically from the argument at each call site -- you rarely need to specify it explicitly, though you can with `first<number>([1, 2, 3])`.

## Multiple type parameters

Generic functions can take more than one type parameter, useful for operations that relate two independent types:

```ts
function pair<A, B>(a: A, b: B): [A, B] {
  return [a, b];
}

const p = pair("age", 30); // p: [string, number]
```

Generics let a single function definition serve infinitely many concrete types, without sacrificing the precision that `any` throws away.

codeExamples.code:
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T {
  return arr[0];
}

const n = first([1, 2, 3]);
const s = first(["a", "b", "c"]);

console.log(n.toFixed(1));
console.log(s.toUpperCase());

function pair<A, B>(a: A, b: B): [A, B] {
  return [a, b];
}

const p = pair("age", 30);
console.log(`${p[0]} = ${p[1]}`);

lessons.contentMarkdown:
By default, a generic type parameter `T` could be *anything*, which means the compiler won't let you assume it has any particular property or method. A **constraint**, written with `extends`, narrows `T` to only types that satisfy a given shape.

## Constraining with extends

```ts
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(value: T): number {
  return value.length;
}

getLength("hello");       // OK -- strings have .length
getLength([1, 2, 3]);     // OK -- arrays have .length
// getLength(42);         // Error: number doesn't have a 'length' property
```

Without the constraint, `value.length` inside the function body would be a compile error, since an unconstrained `T` guarantees nothing.

## Constraining to keys of another type (`keyof`)

A very common pattern is constraining one type parameter using the keys of another, which is the backbone of type-safe property-access helpers:

```ts
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
const name = getProperty(user, "name"); // name: string
// getProperty(user, "email");          // Error: 'email' is not a key of user
```

`K extends keyof T` means "`K` must be one of the literal property names that exist on `T`" -- so passing a key that doesn't exist on the object is caught at compile time, and the return type `T[K]` precisely reflects the type of that specific property.

## Default type parameters

Generics can also have default values, used when the caller doesn't specify one explicitly:

```ts
interface ApiResponse<T = unknown> {
  data: T;
  status: number;
}
```

Constraints and defaults together make generics expressive enough to model real-world APIs precisely, while still catching misuse at compile time.

codeExamples.code:
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(value: T): number {
  return value.length;
}

console.log(getLength("hello"));
console.log(getLength([1, 2, 3, 4]));

function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
const userName = getProperty(user, "name");
const id = getProperty(user, "id");

console.log(`#${id} ${userName}`);

interviewQuestions.answer:
`any` discards type information entirely -- once a value is typed `any`, the compiler stops checking anything about it, and the function's return type (if derived from an `any` parameter) also becomes `any`, silently propagating the loss of safety to every caller.

A generic preserves the *relationship* between the input and output types. For example:

```ts
function wrapAny(value: any): any[] {
  return [value];
}

function wrapGeneric<T>(value: T): T[] {
  return [value];
}

const a = wrapAny(42);       // a: any[] -- no idea what's inside
const b = wrapGeneric(42);   // b: number[] -- precisely known
```

With `wrapGeneric`, calling `.toFixed()` on an element of `b` is safely allowed and checked, while doing the same on `a` compiles without any guarantee it will actually work at runtime. Generics let you write one reusable implementation while still giving every caller full, specific type-checking on both what they pass in and what they get back.

`keyof T` produces a union of string literal types representing every property name that exists on `T`. Writing `K extends keyof T` as a second type parameter's constraint means `K` must be one of those literal key names -- not an arbitrary string.

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const u: User = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
getProperty(u, "name"); // OK, returns string
// getProperty(u, "email"); // Error: Argument of type '"email"' is not assignable to parameter of type '"id" | "name"'.
```

This pattern is useful because it gives you a generic, reusable accessor function that is still fully type-checked: the compiler rejects any key that doesn't actually exist on the object, and it infers the precise return type `T[K]` (an "indexed access type") rather than a generic catch-all like `any` or `unknown`. It is the foundation behind many utility functions and some of TypeScript's own built-in utility types.

`T extends SomeType` is a **constraint**: it restricts which types are legal arguments for `T`, and the compiler rejects any type argument that doesn't satisfy it. It affects what you're *allowed to pass*.

`T = SomeType` (a default) only supplies a **fallback** value for `T` when the caller doesn't specify one and it can't be inferred from the arguments. It affects what happens when you *don't* specify anything, and does not restrict what you're allowed to pass otherwise.

They are commonly combined:

```ts
interface ApiResponse<T extends object = Record<string, unknown>> {
  data: T;
  status: number;
}

// Uses the default:
const r1: ApiResponse = { data: { anything: true }, status: 200 };

// Overrides with a specific, still object-shaped, type:
const r2: ApiResponse<{ id: number }> = { data: { id: 1 }, status: 200 };

// Error: number doesn't satisfy the 'extends object' constraint
// const r3: ApiResponse<number> = { data: 5, status: 200 };
```

A constraint without a default still requires the type argument to be inferable or explicitly provided; a default without a constraint simply widens what "nothing specified" resolves to, while still allowing any type to be passed explicitly.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
TypeScript ships with a set of built-in **utility types** that transform an existing type into a new one, so you don't have to hand-write near-duplicate interfaces for common variations like "all fields optional" or "only some fields".

## Partial\<T\>

`Partial<T>` makes every property of `T` optional. It's commonly used for "patch" or "update" style functions, where a caller only supplies the fields they want to change:

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function updateUser(id: number, changes: Partial<User>): void {
  console.log(`Updating #${id} with`, changes);
}

updateUser(1, { name: "New Name" }); // no need to supply email too
```

## Pick\<T, K\>

`Pick<T, K>` builds a new type containing only the listed keys `K` (a union of string literals) from `T`:

```ts
type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;
// equivalent to: { id: number; name: string }
```

## Omit\<T, K\>

`Omit<T, K>` is the inverse of `Pick`: it builds a new type containing every key of `T` *except* the ones listed:

```ts
type UserWithoutEmail = Omit<User, "email">;
// equivalent to: { id: number; name: string }
```

`Pick` and `Omit` are especially useful for deriving DTOs (data transfer objects) from a single source-of-truth interface, keeping request/response shapes in sync automatically whenever the base interface changes.

codeExamples.code:
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function updateUser(current: User, changes: Partial<User>): User {
  return { ...current, ...changes };
}

const user: User = { id: 1, name: "Alice", email: "alice@old.com" };
const updated = updateUser(user, { email: "alice@new.com" });

console.log(updated.name, updated.email);

interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;
type UserWithoutEmail = Omit<User, "email">;

const preview: UserPreview = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
const noEmail: UserWithoutEmail = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };

console.log(JSON.stringify(preview));
console.log(JSON.stringify(noEmail));

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Beyond `Partial`, `Pick`, and `Omit`, TypeScript includes several other utility types that cover common transformation needs.

## Record\<K, T\>

`Record<K, T>` builds an object type whose keys are `K` and whose values are all of type `T`. It's the type-safe way to describe a dictionary or lookup map:

```ts
type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";

const permissions: Record<Role, string[]> = {
  admin: ["read", "write", "delete"],
  editor: ["read", "write"],
  viewer: ["read"],
};
```

Because `Role` is a union of three literals, the compiler *requires* `permissions` to have exactly those three keys -- missing one, or adding an unlisted key, is a compile error.

## Readonly\<T\>

`Readonly<T>` makes every property of `T` readonly, preventing reassignment after creation:

```ts
interface Point { x: number; y: number; }
const p: Readonly<Point> = { x: 0, y: 0 };
// p.x = 5; // Error: Cannot assign to 'x' because it is a read-only property.
```

## Required\<T\>

`Required<T>` is the inverse of `Partial<T>`: it makes every optional property required:

```ts
interface Options { timeout?: number; retries?: number; }
type StrictOptions = Required<Options>; // both fields now mandatory
```

## ReturnType\<F\> and Parameters\<F\>

These extract type information directly from a function type, useful for staying in sync with a function's signature without manually re-declaring it:

```ts
function createUser(name: string, age: number) {
  return { name, age, createdAt: new Date() };
}

type NewUser = ReturnType<typeof createUser>;
type CreateUserArgs = Parameters<typeof createUser>; // [string, number]
```

Together, these utility types let you derive almost any shape you need from a single, authoritative type definition -- reducing duplication and keeping related types automatically in sync.

codeExamples.code:
type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";

const permissions: Record<Role, string[]> = {
  admin: ["read", "write", "delete"],
  editor: ["read", "write"],
  viewer: ["read"],
};

console.log(permissions.editor.join(", "));

function createUser(name: string, age: number) {
  return { name, age, isActive: true };
}

type NewUser = ReturnType<typeof createUser>;

const u: NewUser = { name: "Sam", age: 28, isActive: false };
console.log(`${u.name} (${u.age}) active=${u.isActive}`);

interviewQuestions.answer:
`Partial<T>` produces a new type with every property of `T` made optional. `Required<T>` does the opposite: it produces a new type with every property made mandatory, even ones that were originally optional.

```ts
interface Settings {
  theme?: string;
  fontSize?: number;
}

type FullSettings = Required<Settings>;
// { theme: string; fontSize: number } -- both now mandatory

function updateSettings(patch: Partial<Settings>) {
  // patch may contain zero or more of the fields
}
```

`Partial<T>` is most useful for "patch"/"update" function parameters, where a caller supplies only the fields they want to change, and for building up an object incrementally before it's complete. `Required<T>` is useful when you need to guarantee every optional field has actually been filled in before proceeding -- for example, after merging user input with a set of defaults, you'd cast or validate the result as `Required<Settings>` to assure the rest of the code that no field is missing anymore.

`Pick<T, K>` builds a new type containing *only* the properties listed in `K` (a union of `T`'s key names). `Omit<T, K>` builds a new type containing *every* property of `T` *except* the ones listed in `K`. They're complementary: `Pick` is an allowlist, `Omit` is a denylist.

Given:

```ts
interface Product {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  description: string;
}
```

To derive a "create" DTO that excludes the server-generated `id`:

```ts
type CreateProductDto = Omit<Product, "id">;

function createProduct(dto: CreateProductDto): Product {
  return { id: Date.now(), ...dto };
}
```

`Omit` is the natural choice here because the goal is "everything except one or two known fields" -- using `Pick` would require listing every *remaining* field explicitly (`Pick<Product, "name" | "price" | "description">`), which is more verbose and has to be updated every time a new field is added to `Product`. As a rule of thumb: use `Omit` when excluding a small, known set of fields from a large type; use `Pick` when selecting a small, known subset from a large type.

`Record<K, T>` is defined (conceptually) as a mapped type: `{ [P in K]: T }`. When `K` is a union of string literal types, this expands to an object type with exactly those keys as required properties, each typed `T`. Because every member of the union becomes a *required* property in the resulting type, TypeScript's normal object-literal checking applies: an object literal assigned to that `Record` must supply every one of those keys, and supplying an extra key not in `K` is rejected as an excess property.

```ts
type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";

const labels: Record<Role, string> = {
  admin: "Administrator",
  editor: "Editor",
  // viewer: "Viewer", // omitted
};
// Error: Property 'viewer' is missing in type '{ admin: string; editor: string; }'
// but required in type 'Record<Role, string>'.
```

This makes `Record` a lightweight, built-in way to get compiler-enforced exhaustiveness whenever you need a value for every member of a known, closed set of string literals -- functionally similar to writing a `switch` with a `never`-typed default case, but expressed as a data shape instead of control flow. It's commonly used for things like per-status labels, per-role permission sets, or per-locale translation tables, where adding a new literal to the union immediately surfaces every object that now needs updating.

