TypeScript classes build on JavaScript's `class` syntax, adding type annotations for fields, constructor parameters, and method signatures.

## Declaring a class

```ts
class Account {
  balance: number;

  constructor(initialBalance: number) {
    this.balance = initialBalance;
  }

  deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
  }
}

const acc = new Account(100);
acc.deposit(50);
console.log(acc.balance); // 150
```

## Parameter properties

Writing `constructor(public balance: number)` directly declares *and* assigns a class field in one step, eliminating boilerplate:

```ts
class Account {
  constructor(public balance: number) {}

  deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
  }
}
```

This is equivalent to the first example but removes the need to separately declare `balance: number` and write `this.balance = initialBalance` -- the access modifier on a constructor parameter tells TypeScript to do both automatically.

## Methods and `this`

Methods are declared like regular functions inside the class body, and `this` refers to the instance they were called on, exactly as in JavaScript. Arrow-function class fields (`deposit = (amount: number) => { ... }`) are a common pattern when a method will be passed as a callback and needs a permanently bound `this`.

codeExamples.code:
class Account {
  balance: number;

  constructor(initialBalance: number) {
    this.balance = initialBalance;
  }

  deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
  }
}

const acc = new Account(100);
acc.deposit(50);
console.log(acc.balance);

class Account {
  constructor(public balance: number) {}

  deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
  }
}

const acc = new Account(200);
acc.deposit(25);
console.log(acc.balance);

