**Narrowing** is the process by which TypeScript refines a broad type (like a union) down to a more specific one, based on a runtime check it recognizes. TypeScript ships with built-in understanding of several common JavaScript runtime checks, automatically narrowing types within the branch where the check holds true.

## typeof

The classic JavaScript `typeof` operator narrows primitive types:

```ts
function describe(value: string | number): string {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    return value.toUpperCase(); // value: string
  }
  return value.toFixed(2); // value: number
}
```

## instanceof

`instanceof` narrows based on a value's prototype chain, useful for distinguishing between class instances:

```ts
class Dog { bark() { return "Woof"; } }
class Cat { meow() { return "Meow"; } }

function speak(animal: Dog | Cat): string {
  if (animal instanceof Dog) {
    return animal.bark(); // animal: Dog
  }
  return animal.meow(); // animal: Cat
}
```

## in

The `in` operator narrows based on whether an object has a particular property, useful for unions of object shapes that don't share a discriminant tag:

```ts
interface Bird { fly(): string; }
interface Fish { swim(): string; }

function move(animal: Bird | Fish): string {
  if ("fly" in animal) {
    return animal.fly(); // animal: Bird
  }
  return animal.swim(); // animal: Fish
}
```

## Truthiness and equality narrowing

Simple truthy/falsy checks (`if (value)`), and strict equality checks against `null`/`undefined` or specific literal values, also narrow types automatically -- TypeScript tracks control flow and updates a variable's known type after every conditional branch.

codeExamples.code:
function describe(value: string | number): string {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    return value.toUpperCase();
  }
  return value.toFixed(2);
}

console.log(describe("hello"));
console.log(describe(3.14159));

class Dog {
  bark(): string { return "Woof"; }
}
class Cat {
  meow(): string { return "Meow"; }
}

function speak(animal: Dog | Cat): string {
  if (animal instanceof Dog) {
    return animal.bark();
  }
  return animal.meow();
}

interface Bird { fly(): string; }
interface Fish { swim(): string; }

function move(animal: Bird | Fish): string {
  if ("fly" in animal) {
    return animal.fly();
  }
  return animal.swim();
}

console.log(speak(new Dog()));
console.log(move({ swim: () => "Swimming" }));

lessons.contentMarkdown:
When the built-in narrowing mechanisms (`typeof`, `instanceof`, `in`) aren't enough to express a check you need -- for example, validating a complex shape at runtime -- you can write your own **user-defined type guard function**.

## The `is` type predicate

A type guard function is a regular function whose return type is a special **type predicate**: `parameterName is SomeType`. This tells the compiler: "if this function returns `true`, narrow the argument to `SomeType` everywhere after the check."

```ts
interface Cat {
  meow(): string;
}
interface Dog {
  bark(): string;
}

function isCat(animal: Cat | Dog): animal is Cat {
  return typeof (animal as Cat).meow === "function";
}

function speak(animal: Cat | Dog): string {
  if (isCat(animal)) {
    return animal.meow(); // animal narrowed to Cat
  }
  return animal.bark(); // animal narrowed to Dog
}
```

Without the `animal is Cat` return type (if it were just a plain `boolean`), the compiler would have no way to connect the function's result back to a narrowing effect on the argument -- it would treat `isCat(animal)` like any other boolean-returning call, with no special narrowing behavior in the branches of an `if`.

## Narrowing with unknown data (validating external input)

Custom type guards are especially valuable for safely narrowing `unknown` data -- such as a parsed JSON payload from an API -- into a specific, trusted shape:

```ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return (
    typeof value === "object" &&
    value !== null &&
    typeof (value as User).id === "number" &&
    typeof (value as User).name === "string"
  );
}

function processPayload(payload: unknown): string {
  if (isUser(payload)) {
    return `User #${payload.id}: ${payload.name}`;
  }
  return "Invalid payload";
}
```

This pattern combines runtime validation (actually checking the shape of unknown data) with compile-time safety (the type system trusting that validation to narrow `unknown` into `User`), closing the gap between what TypeScript assumes and what's actually true at runtime.

codeExamples.code:
interface Cat {
  meow(): string;
}
interface Dog {
  bark(): string;
}

function isCat(animal: Cat | Dog): animal is Cat {
  return typeof (animal as Cat).meow === "function";
}

function speak(animal: Cat | Dog): string {
  if (isCat(animal)) {
    return animal.meow();
  }
  return animal.bark();
}

const cat: Cat = { meow: () => "Meow" };
const dog: Dog = { bark: () => "Woof" };

console.log(speak(cat));
console.log(speak(dog));

interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return (
    typeof value === "object" &&
    value !== null &&
    typeof (value as User).id === "number" &&
    typeof (value as User).name === "string"
  );
}

function processPayload(payload: unknown): string {
  if (isUser(payload)) {
    return `User #${payload.id}: ${payload.name}`;
  }
  return "Invalid payload";
}

console.log(processPayload({ id: 1, name: "Alice" }));
console.log(processPayload("not a user"));

interviewQuestions.answer:
A user-defined type guard is a function whose return type is a **type predicate** -- written as `parameterName is SomeType` -- instead of a plain `boolean`. Calling this function inside a conditional tells the compiler to narrow the checked argument to `SomeType` within the branch where the function returned `true` (and, in the `else` branch, to exclude `SomeType` from the original union, if applicable).

```ts
interface Admin {
  role: "admin";
  permissions: string[];
}
interface Guest {
  role: "guest";
}

function isAdmin(user: Admin | Guest): user is Admin {
  return user.role === "admin";
}

function describe(user: Admin | Guest): string {
  if (isAdmin(user)) {
    return `Admin with ${user.permissions.length} permissions`; // narrowed to Admin
  }
  return "Guest user";
}
```

This is needed whenever the logic required to distinguish between types is too complex, reusable, or abstracted-away to express as a single inline `typeof`/`instanceof`/`in` check at every call site -- for example, validating a complex nested shape, checking several conditions together, or reusing the same check across many different functions. Without the `is` predicate (if `isAdmin` just returned a plain `boolean`), the compiler would treat the call like any ordinary boolean-returning function and would **not** narrow `user` in either branch, forcing callers to manually re-assert the type themselves.

No -- TypeScript does **not** verify that a type guard's return type predicate (`param is SomeType`) actually, logically corresponds to what the function's body checks. The compiler simply **trusts** the annotation at every call site. This means it's entirely possible to write a type guard that returns `true` under conditions that don't actually guarantee the claimed type, and TypeScript will still narrow accordingly -- creating a type-safety hole that looks exactly like safe, checked code.

```ts
interface Cat { meow(): string; }
interface Dog { bark(): string; }

// Deliberately wrong: always returns true, regardless of the actual shape!
function isCat(animal: Cat | Dog): animal is Cat {
  return true; // BUG: no real check at all
}

function speak(animal: Cat | Dog): string {
  if (isCat(animal)) {
    return animal.meow(); // compiler trusts this is a Cat...
  }
  return animal.bark();
}

const notActuallyACat = { bark: () => "Woof" } as unknown as Dog;
// speak(notActuallyACat) would crash at runtime: animal.meow is not a function
```

The risk is that a buggy or incomplete type guard provides a false sense of safety: the code compiles cleanly, reads as though it's been properly validated, and yet can still crash at runtime exactly the way untyped JavaScript would. This makes type guards a place worth extra scrutiny in code review and testing -- unlike most TypeScript features, an incorrect type guard is a silent, unchecked claim rather than something the compiler can catch for you. Thorough unit tests covering the guard's actual runtime behavior (not just its type-level usage) are the main mitigation.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A **mapped type** builds a new object type by iterating over the keys of an existing type and applying a transformation to each one. This is exactly how built-in utility types like `Partial<T>` and `Readonly<T>` are implemented under the hood.

## The basic syntax

```ts
type OptionsFlags<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]: boolean;
};

interface Features {
  darkMode: () => void;
  newDashboard: () => void;
}

type FeatureFlags = OptionsFlags<Features>;
// equivalent to: { darkMode: boolean; newDashboard: boolean }
```

`[K in keyof T]` iterates over every key `K` of `T`, and `: boolean` sets every resulting property's type to `boolean`, discarding the original property types entirely.

## Modifiers: readonly and optional

Mapped types can add or remove the `readonly` and `?` (optional) modifiers using `+`/`-` prefixes:

```ts
type MyPartial<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]?: T[K];
};

type MyRequired<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]-?: T[K]; // the -? strips optionality
};

type MyReadonly<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K];
};
```

This is literally how TypeScript's lib definitions implement `Partial`, `Required`, and `Readonly` -- they are mapped types over `keyof T`, nothing more exotic.

## Key remapping with `as`

Modern TypeScript supports renaming keys during the mapping, using an `as` clause -- useful for programmatically deriving getter-style names, for example:

```ts
type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};

interface Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

type PersonGetters = Getters<Person>;
// { getName: () => string; getAge: () => number }
```

Mapped types let you express "take this shape and transform every property the same way" once, generically, instead of hand-writing a new, nearly-identical interface every time you need a variant.

codeExamples.code:
type OptionsFlags<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]: boolean;
};

interface Features {
  darkMode: () => void;
  newDashboard: () => void;
}

type FeatureFlags = OptionsFlags<Features>;

const flags: FeatureFlags = {
  darkMode: true,
  newDashboard: false,
};

console.log(flags.darkMode, flags.newDashboard);

type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};

interface Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

const personGetters: Getters<Person> = {
  getName: () => "Alice",
  getAge: () => 30,
};

console.log(personGetters.getName(), personGetters.getAge());

type UnwrapPromise<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T;

async function fetchName(): Promise<string> {
  return "Alice";
}

type NameType = UnwrapPromise<ReturnType<typeof fetchName>>; // string

const resolvedName: NameType = "Bob";
console.log(resolvedName.toUpperCase());

type NonNullableCustom<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;

function printValue<T>(value: NonNullableCustom<T>): void {
  console.log(value);
}

printValue<string>("hello");
printValue<number>(42);

interviewQuestions.answer:
A mapped type generates a new object type by iterating over the keys of an existing type (via `[K in keyof T]`) and applying the same transformation to each resulting property. It's a way to express "take this shape, and change every property the same way" as a single reusable, generic type, instead of writing a new interface by hand for every variant you need.

`Partial<T>`, `Required<T>`, and `Readonly<T>` are not special compiler magic -- they are themselves implemented as ordinary mapped types in TypeScript's own standard library definitions:

```ts
type Partial<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]?: T[P];
};

type Readonly<T> = {
  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];
};

type Required<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]-?: T[P]; // -? strips the optional modifier
};
```

Understanding this means you can write your own custom variants just as easily -- for example, a `Nullable<T>` that makes every property's type include `null`:

```ts
type Nullable<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null;
};
```

This demonstrates that TypeScript's utility types aren't a fixed, closed set -- they're simply common, pre-written examples of a general-purpose, learnable pattern.

`infer` can only appear inside the `extends` clause of a conditional type. It introduces a new type variable whose value is inferred from matching the checked type's structure against a pattern -- essentially "if this larger type fits this shape, capture *this specific part* of it under a new name I can use in the true branch."

```ts
type MyReturnType<F> = F extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;

function greet(): string {
  return "hi";
}

type G = MyReturnType<typeof greet>; // string
```

Here, `F extends (...args: any[]) => infer R` checks whether `F` is some kind of function type, and if so, captures whatever its return type happens to be into `R`, making it available in the `? R` branch. If `F` isn't a function type at all, the condition fails and the `: never` branch is used instead.

This is exactly the mechanism TypeScript's real, built-in `ReturnType<F>` is implemented with. The same technique powers `Parameters<F>` (inferring the parameter tuple instead of the return type), and `Awaited<T>` (inferring and recursively unwrapping a `Promise`'s resolved value type). `infer` is the core tool that turns conditional types from a simple type-level if/else into a genuinely powerful type-level pattern-matching and extraction mechanism.

When a conditional type checks a **bare, naked type parameter** (one that appears directly, not wrapped in an array, tuple, object, or other structure) against `extends`, and that type parameter is instantiated with a union, TypeScript applies the conditional type to **each member of the union individually**, then unions the results back together. This behavior is called distribution.

```ts
type ToArray<T> = T extends any ? T[] : never;

type Result = ToArray<string | number>;
// Distributes as: ToArray<string> | ToArray<number>
// = string[] | number[]
// NOT (string | number)[] !
```

This can be surprising if you expected the union to be treated as one single type being checked all at once -- distribution instead splits it apart, runs the conditional separately for each branch, and re-combines the (possibly differently-shaped) results.

To **suppress** distribution and force the union to be checked as a single, whole type, wrap both the checked type and the `extends` clause's type in a tuple (or any other non-bare container):

```ts
type ToArrayNonDistributive<T> = [T] extends [any] ? T[] : never;

type Result2 = ToArrayNonDistributive<string | number>;
// (string | number)[]  -- the union is NOT split apart
```

This distinction matters in practice whenever a conditional type's behavior needs to differ between "process each union member independently" (the default, distributive behavior) versus "treat the whole union as one indivisible type" (achieved by wrapping in a tuple to opt out of distribution) -- getting it backwards produces a subtly wrong resulting type that's easy to miss until something downstream fails to compile.

